THE SAME TWO CHANGES ON aarch64-darwin — AND THE ISA IS WORTH 0.17x ON THE
INTER ARM.

date:   2026-09-03
host:   mac — Apple silicon, macOS (16 KiB pages, libmalloc magazine
        allocator). `/usr/bin/time -l` maximum resident set size, MEDIAN OF 5.
        There is no heaptrack on macOS, so **no heap number appears in this
        file**; every figure here is RSS.
trees:  base  = 01ef6f1f1 (main)
        after = base + the one-byte `MeCandidate` commit + the `perf_encode`
                one-copy harness commit
data:   benchmarks/mem_aarch64_2026-09-03.tsv
        x86 sibling: benchmarks/mem_harness_2026-09-03.tsv (same harness, same
        grid, same two trees)

=================== 1. THE CHANGES CARRY ACROSS, BY MORE THAN ON x86 =========

  size  arm         base    after    delta    C     base/C  after/C
  1280  still      38128    35760    -2368   45392   0.840   0.788
  1280  videokey   44272    41824    -2448   47568   0.930   0.879
  1280  inter      72160    59520   -12640   53072   1.360   1.121
  1536  inter     102960    88048   -14912   72048   1.430   1.222
  1920  inter     156656   132224   -24432  106752   1.467   1.239
  2048  still      87712    81520    -6192   95824   0.916   0.851
  2048  videokey  107600    96192   -11408  106816   1.007   0.901
  2048  inter     177216   150080   -27136  119424   1.483   1.257

**The inter arm loses 15-18 % of its peak RSS at every size**, and **eleven of
the twelve cells are now inside the 25 % goal**. The twelfth — 2048x2048
inter — is 1.257x, which is over the line by 0.007 and inside the port's own
run-to-run spread on that cell ([149936, 157376], 5 %). Call it AT the boundary,
not inside it.

Attribution between the two commits, measured separately on this host at p13:
the `MeCandidate` packing alone is -3552 KiB at 1280 inter and -8816 at 2048
inter, and **0 on the still and videokey arms** (+64 to +176 KiB, i.e. noise) —
the same control the x86 record has. The rest is the harness change.

=================== 2. THE ISA IS WORTH MORE THAN EITHER CHANGE ==============

Same commits, same cells, same harness, two hosts:

  2048x2048 inter, after both changes
    aarch64-darwin  peak RSS  150080 KiB (153.7 MB)   port/C 1.257
    x86_64-linux    peak RSS  114924 KiB (117.7 MB)   port/C 1.086
    x86_64-linux    peak HEAP            112.73 MB

**36 MB of the aarch64 number is not live bytes.** On Linux the port's peak RSS
sits 2.2 MB above its peak heap on this cell; on macOS it sits ~37 MB above the
same encode's heap, if the heap is close to ISA-invariant — which is likely
(the allocation SIZES are the same code) but is NOT MEASURED, because heaptrack
does not exist on macOS. Treat "37 MB" as bounded above by "the aarch64 RSS
minus the x86 heap", not as an attributed quantity.

Whatever it is, it is not something a lifetime change can reach: the two
changes here removed ~27 MB of live bytes on aarch64 and the residual gap to C
is still 30 MB. **The next thing to measure on this axis is whether allocator
CHURN moves macOS RSS**, which nothing in this repo has tested. The three
allocation-site hoists of 2026-09-03 (`58fa779e`, `fbd341b3`, `0c70f3fc`) are
the obvious A/B for it: they were measured as a CPU win and a peak-HEAP null
(`benchmarks/mem_heaptrack_satd_2026-09-03.meta`), and a peak-heap null is NOT
a peak-RSS null on an allocator that does not return freed pages. That
measurement has not been made and this file does not make it.

=================== 3. WHAT THIS SAYS ABOUT THE RECORDED 1.60x ===============

`benchmarks/mem_arms_2026-09-02.meta` records the inter arm at 1.60x at 4 MP on
aarch64. This file reads **1.483x on `main`** at 4 MP on aarch64 — same ISA,
same quantity, close enough that the difference is plausibly the day's commits
and the C driver (that record used `capture_c_trace`; this uses
`perf_c_encode`). So the aarch64 series is broadly reproducible and it is the
**x86 reading of 0.84x at p6** (benchmarks/mem_preset_2026-09-03.meta) that is
the outlier — i.e. the conflict recorded there is an ISA conflict, not a
harness one. Neither host has been measured with the other's C driver.

=================== NOT ESTABLISHED ==========================================
  * Any aarch64 HEAP number. No heaptrack on macOS.
  * Any preset but 13, content but gradient, qp but 40, size outside 1280-2048.
  * That the 36 MB aarch64-over-x86 RSS gap is allocator retention. It is
    UNATTRIBUTED; page size (16 KiB vs 4 KiB), the malloc implementation, and
    thread-stack accounting are all candidates and none has been isolated.
  * That the 2048 inter cell meets the goal. 1.257x is over 1.25x.
